    "q-1661": {
      "dom_id": "step-q1661",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "If the hypotenuse of a ${45}^{∘}$-${45}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle has a length of $9$, then what is the length of each leg?",
      "worked_solution": "The hypotenuse in a ${45}^{∘}$-${45}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, we know that the hypotenuse has length $9.$ So, if the length of a leg is $a,$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a \\\\ a & =\\frac{9}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the square root from the denominator, we can multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{9\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is $\\frac{9\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
