      "source_example_id": "e-3080",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Length of the Legs of a 45-45-90 Triangle Given the Hypotenuse",
      "kp_id": "e-3080"
    },
    "q-53421": {
      "dom_id": "step-q53421",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14182820/assets/a789720bf1dca7c32369d21c2eb2760b0de5524b49a489358df88c97ac7c137a.png)\n\nWhat is the length of one leg of the triangle above?",
      "worked_solution": "The hypotenuse in a ${45}^{∘}$-${45}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg.\n\nHere, we know that the hypotenuse has length $8.$ So, if the length of a leg is $a,$ then we must have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8 & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot a \\\\ a & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the square root from the denominator, we can multiply both the numerator and denominator by $\\sqrt{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{8\\cdot \\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of each leg is $4\\sqrt{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
