    "q-20303": {
      "dom_id": "step-q20303",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Find the perimeter of a square whose diagonal is $\\frac{48}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,mm.$",
      "worked_solution": "A diagram of the given situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14182820/assets/5844e63004f2c12f7af15b2fe178a3e48f67e63d06692db532e5261d3f9f7df6.png)\n\nNotice that $△FHG$ is a ${45}^{∘}$-${45}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle since $HG=GF$ and $m∠HGF={90}^{∘}.$\n\nThe hypotenuse in an isosceles right triangle is equal to $\\sqrt{2}$ times the length of a leg. \n\nHere, the length of the hypotenuse $\\overset{―}{FH}$ is $\\frac{48}{\\sqrt{2}}\\,mm.$ So the length of the leg $\\overset{―}{HG}$ can be found by solving the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}FH & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot HG \\\\ \\frac{48}{\\sqrt{2}} & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot HG \\\\ HG & =\\frac{48}{\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2}} \\\\ HG & =\\frac{48}{2} \\\\ HG & =24\\,mm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the perimeter $p$ is given by\n\n$$\np=4\\cdot HG=4\\cdot 24=96\\,mm.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
